1. What It Is
A provenance-aware, versioned graph of legal cases and the precedents they establish. Each case is a node. Each precedent is a directed edge from the case that establishes it to the case that follows it, or from the case that follows it to the case that establishes it. The Legal Precedent Graph records each case with its citation, its court, its date, its jurisdiction, its holding, the precedents it establishes, the precedents it follows, the precedents it distinguishes, and the cases that overrule it or are overruled by it.

It reuses:

the STAG Schema Library (App 17)

the STAG Graph Viewer (App 5)

the Provenance-Aware Annotation Database (App 20)

the PROV-O Exporter (App 3)

the Revision History Viewer (App 7)

the Numerical Verification Tool (App 22)

the Physics Knowledge Library (App 23)

the Autofill Library Service (App 49)

It is for lawyers, judges, legal scholars, law students, and anyone who works with legal precedent.

2. Core Requirements
Functional requirements:

Accept deposits of legal cases from users.

Store each case with its citation, its court, its date, its jurisdiction, and its provenance.

Record the holding: the legal rule the case establishes.

Record the precedents it establishes: the rules that later cases must follow.

Record the precedents it follows: the earlier cases it relies on.

Record the precedents it distinguishes: the earlier cases it declines to follow, with the reason.

Record the precedents it overrules: the earlier cases it declares invalid.

Record the cases that overrule it.

Record the relation type: followed, distinguished, overruled, doubted, criticised, applied, considered, or other.

Record the strength of the precedent: binding, persuasive, or not_assessed.

Record the jurisdiction and the court hierarchy.

Record the subject matter: the area of law.

Record the key phrases and the key rules.

Enforce provenance completeness.

Enforce versioning.

Enforce supersession acyclicity.

Support querying by case ID, citation, court, date, jurisdiction, subject matter, holding, precedent type, precedent strength, depositor, deposit date, and keywords.

Support full-text search of the case summary, the holding, and the notes.

Support aggregation queries: count by court, by jurisdiction, by subject matter, by year, by precedent type.

Support exporting a case as JSON, PROV-O, and Turtle.

Support importing a case from JSON.

Support bulk import and bulk export.

Support a REST API for CRUD operations.

Support a GraphQL API for flexible queries.

Support a command-line interface for administration.

Support authentication and authorization.

Support audit logging.

Support backup and restore.

Support a web interface.

Support moderation.

Support linking a case to a Physics Knowledge Library work if the case is described in a deposited work.

Support the precedent graph: a visualisation of the cases as nodes and the precedents as edges.

Support the citation network: a visualisation of the citation relationships between cases.

Support the precedent timeline: a visualisation of the precedents over time.

Support the jurisdiction map: a visualisation of the cases by jurisdiction and court hierarchy.

Non-functional requirements:

Deterministic.

Fast: query a typical case in under 100 milliseconds.

Scalable: support up to 10^6 cases.

Versioned: the graph records the STAG schema version, the legal case schema version, and the graph version.

Reproducible.

Portable.

Well-documented.

